1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,112
So far we've treated theta as one long vector.

2
00:00:09,112 --> 00:00:12,975
But a network's weights come as matrices, one for each layer.

3
00:00:12,975 --> 00:00:18,673
And every scaling problem we met in Xavier initialization came from those weight matrices.

4
00:00:18,673 --> 00:00:24,300
So let's measure a step as what it really is, which is a matrix.

5
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,859
First we need an inner product for matrices.

6
00:00:26,859 --> 00:00:31,745
Multiply the entries in matching spots and add everything up, which here gives five.

7
00:00:31,745 --> 00:00:37,833
That's the same number you get from the trace of G transpose times the step.

8
00:00:37,833 --> 00:00:39,124
How big is a matrix?

9
00:00:39,124 --> 00:00:45,517
Take this two by two matrix, and feed it every input of length one, which is the whole unit circle.

10
00:00:45,517 --> 00:00:48,733
What comes out the other side is an ellipse.

11
00:00:48,733 --> 00:00:53,689
Its longest stretch is two point one four, and that's the largest singular value.

12
00:00:53,689 --> 00:00:55,891
The shortest is zero point six nine.

13
00:00:55,891 --> 00:01:02,560
The biggest stretch is what we call the spectral norm, so it's the most the matrix can stretch a unit vector.

14
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:10,300
That makes the spectral ball of radius eta the set of steps that stretch no input by more than eta.

15
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:12,549
Now ask our question for a matrix.

16
00:01:12,549 --> 00:01:17,049
Which step of spectral size eta lowers the linearized loss the most?

17
00:01:17,049 --> 00:01:21,748
To answer it, break the gradient into its singular value decomposition.

18
00:01:21,748 --> 00:01:28,000
That gives us output directions U, input directions V, and a stretch sigma for each pair.

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,010
The trace is linear, so the inner product splits into a sum, with each singular value times one number.

20
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:40,455
Each of those numbers is at most one, because B can't stretch anything past one.

21
00:01:40,455 --> 00:01:46,580
So the sum of the sigmas is a ceiling, and choosing B to be U V transpose hits it exactly.

22
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:51,900
That makes the best step minus eta times U V transpose.

23
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:53,767
Let's check that with numbers.

24
00:01:53,767 --> 00:01:56,069
Take a random five by three gradient.

25
00:01:56,069 --> 00:02:01,857
Its singular values are three point five four, two point one five and zero point seven seven.

26
00:02:01,857 --> 00:02:04,900
Together they add up to six point four six.

27
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:08,014
The gradient step collects only four point two one.

28
00:02:08,014 --> 00:02:11,984
And twenty thousand random tries never get past four point eight.

29
00:02:11,984 --> 00:02:14,488
Only U V transpose collects the full sum.

30
00:02:14,488 --> 00:02:20,100
So a bigger ball of allowed steps buys us a bigger predicted drop.

31
00:02:20,100 --> 00:02:22,061
Now look at what this step does.

32
00:02:22,061 --> 00:02:25,371
The gradient has three very different singular values.

33
00:02:25,371 --> 00:02:32,233
But U V transpose keeps the directions and throws the stretches away, so every singular value becomes one.

34
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:34,817
A matrix like that is called semi-orthogonal.

35
00:02:34,817 --> 00:02:38,893
Its condition number drops from four point six all the way down to one.

36
00:02:38,893 --> 00:02:46,766
That means every direction gets the same size of step, and the big singular values don't get to hog it.

37
00:02:46,766 --> 00:02:47,966
And this isn't new.

38
00:02:47,966 --> 00:02:53,836
It's the Shampoo optimizer, from Gupta and coauthors in twenty seventeen and twenty eighteen.

39
00:02:53,836 --> 00:03:00,966
Shampoo squeezes the gradient from both sides with inverse fourth roots, and out comes U V transpose again.

40
00:03:00,966 --> 00:03:06,643
A variant of it, from Dahl and coauthors, won the AlgoPerf contest in twenty twenty-three.

41
00:03:06,643 --> 00:03:08,536
There are two catches, though.

42
00:03:08,536 --> 00:03:16,200
An SVD at every step is expensive, and we still don't know what step size fits layers of different shapes.

